TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Preliminary Report (Part 1)","overview":"Plan a simple space mission that benefits Australia and present preliminary design work to guide the full mission assignment. Explain the mission concept and rationale, plan how the assignment will be completed, identify information sources and software, and show evidence of technical progress."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Mission concept and rationale — What will it do, who are the users, why is it needed?","weight":40,"max":40,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"A clear and complete description of mission goals, objectives, and requirements. Clearly relates required mission function to users and their needs. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Sufficient description of mission goals, objectives, and requirements. Identification of users and their needs. Descriptions feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"A poor description with mission goals, objectives and requirements unclear. Insufficient identification of users and their needs."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Project plan — How you plan to complete the assignment, including either a Gantt chart or a timetable and information sources you plan to use","weight":30,"max":30,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The plan for project completion is robust and has provision for project variations and contingencies. The plan fits within the narrative set out by the mission concept – the student makes clear why the plan is developed this way in the context of the mission. Appropriate references to information sources are included."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Plan is presented but lacks some detail. However, the plan is enough to believe that the project is feasible."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Plan is not included or does not have sufficient detail to demonstrate that the project can be completed."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Preliminary progress — Technical content and evidence of sufficient work done to date","weight":10,"max":10,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Good preliminary set of results included with brief description and/or interpretation. Clearly on track for completion."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Minor work completed on the project. Results description or interpretation may be insufficient."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful work completed."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":20,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical structure. The report is very easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Mission Concept & Rationale"},{"id":"R2","met":"partial","where":"Project Plan / Preliminary Progress"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan, Figure 1"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan, Table 2"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Preliminary Progress, Table 3"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","issue":"Reference [6] (NOAA climate-change page) is cited as the source for the Bureau of Meteorology combining imagery with SST products; citation does not support the claim."},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","issue":"Objective 2 is to be refined against [4], but [4] is a Nature news article, not the primary Asner method paper; the 10 m GSD requirement is therefore not traceable to a technical source."},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","issue":"'Oceans have become 30 percent more acidic' is a simplification; the underlying change is a ~0.1 pH-unit drop (~30% rise in H+ concentration)."},{"where":"Preliminary Progress","issue":"The 97.8 deg sun-synchronous inclination is stated without showing the J2 nodal-precession calculation, so the key result is unverifiable from the report."},{"where":"Preliminary Progress","issue":"The 60 km swath assumes 6,000 cross-track pixels but no detector/pixel-size justification is given, and the 250 km reef width is not referenced."}],"overall":"Strong, well-structured preliminary report. Mission concept, users and requirements are clear and referenced; the Gantt chart, contingencies and source table form a credible plan; hand calculations are correct and interpreted. Main gaps are a few citation-content mismatches and limited STK work to date.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":36.0,"max":40.0,"reasoning":"Clear goal, four numbered objectives/requirements, and named users (AIMS, GBRMPA, CSIRO, BoM) with needs explicitly mapped to objectives. Rationale is well referenced. Minor: [6] is a NOAA page cited for BoM SST products, and [4] is a news article rather than the primary method paper.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","quote":"Give reef managers direct, repeatable observations of coral colour over the whole Great Barrier Reef, so that bleaching is detected within days"},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","quote":"The satellite would be operated by the Australian Institute of Marine Science (AIMS)."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":27.0,"max":30.0,"reasoning":"Gantt chart with completed/planned coding and deadlines, per-task descriptions, a risk/contingency table with triggers and responses, and a sources table. Plan is explicitly justified against the mission sequence (orbit before comms, EOL after orbit). Robust and feasible.","evidence":[{"where":"Project Plan","quote":"The order follows the mission: the imaging requirements (objectives 1 and 2) fix the orbit altitude and local time"},{"where":"Project Plan","quote":"Week 10 is float as well as editing time: no new design work is planned in it."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Hand calculations for a, T, orbits/day, SSO inclination, ground-track shift and swath are all numerically correct (T=5801 s, 14.9 rev/day, 24.2 deg, 60 km). Interpretation identifies the swath/coverage trade and links it to a contingency. STK work is only just started, so progress is good but early.","evidence":[{"where":"Preliminary Progress","quote":"the reef is up to about 250 km wide, so a 60 km swath needs at least five neighbouring passes"},{"where":"Preliminary Progress","quote":"A first STK scenario with this orbit has been set up, and its period agrees with the hand calculation."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":18.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Logical structure, clear headings, professional prose, consistent formatting. Figure 1 and Tables 1-3 are labelled and captioned. All eleven references are cited in text and appear in the list in a consistent style. Only minor citation-content mismatches.","evidence":[{"where":"Project Plan","quote":"Table 2 lists the sources I plan to use and what each is for."},{"where":"References","quote":"[7] J. R. Wertz, D. F. Everett, and J. J. Puschell, Eds., Space Mission Engineering: The New SMAD."}]}]}
